# Line endings.
#
# `text=auto` governs the OBJECT STORE: normalise to LF on commit, convert to
# the platform convention on checkout. Every blob here is already LF, so this
# renormalises nothing today. Its value is that the rule stops depending on
# whichever global core.autocrlf happens to be set -- a checkout on a machine
# with core.autocrlf=false could otherwise start committing CRLF blobs, and
# nothing in the repo would object.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not silence "LF will be replaced by CRLF the
# next time Git touches it". That is core.autocrlf=true finding an LF
# working-tree copy, and it appears whenever a tool writes LF into a CRLF
# working tree -- knitr, roxygen2 and R's writeLines() all do. It is cosmetic:
# the stored blob is LF either way, and the notice clears once the file has
# been committed and checked out again.
#
# Do not reach for `eol=lf` to silence it. That was tried during the 0.10.0
# work and it does not fix the warning, it inverts it -- git then reports
# "CRLF will be replaced by LF" and rewrites the working-tree copy on the next
# checkout. Trading a harmless notice for a real change to every generated file
# is the wrong way round.
* text=auto

# Byte-for-byte round trip; never transform these.
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
*.rds binary

# Text, stated rather than sniffed from content.
*.R      text
*.Rd     text
*.Rmd    text
*.stan   text
*.svg    text
*.md     text
*.yml    text